  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 221 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nTonight through Sunday...\n\nCloud cover will continue to increase across our CWA later tonight \nthanks to increasing mid level moisture. Near term guidance \ncontinues to show an upper shortwave in Texas continue to push \neastward towards the Mid MS Valley. Looking ahead into the overnight \nperiod, weather conditions will remain quiet. Nighttime temperatures \nwill dip into the upper 50s/low 60s areawide. As we head into early \nSaturday morning, scattered light showers will begin to occur mainly \nwest of I-55. Although HREF fog guidance is beginning to show low \nprobabilities (less than 5%) of patchy fog mainly across portions of \nsoutheast AR and all of our northeast LA parishes, there is not \nenough support from the REFS to suggest widespread fog potential. \nFog graphics will not be introduced at this time given the low \nprobability of fog development highlighted by HREF dense fog \nguidance, however shallow patchy fog will be possible particularly \nafter dawn. \n\nHeading into late Saturday morning, scattered light convection will \nspread across the whole area. Light rain showers will continue \nacross the area Saturday afternoon and we may see enough instability \nfor a few heavier showers or a thunderstorm. Scattered convection \nwill continue through Sunday, tapering off Sunday evening. No severe \nweather is expected. Taking a quick glance at the rainfall totals, \nmost of our area will see rain totals below a half inch, some \nlocalized areas could see totals over one inch. With that in mind, \nsome brief heavy downpours cannot be ruled out for a few areas. \nShowers will taper off Sunday evening. Rain may disrupt Halloween \noutdoor plans in some areas Saturday evening, but many areas will \nremain dry during peak candy hunting time. Just be sure to keep a \nclose eye on the weather before venturing out, and have a backup \nplan if needed. /CR/\n\nMonday through Friday...\n\nThe forecast messaging for the long term period remains the same \nwith no major changes made. Long term guidance continues to show an \nupper trough deepening across the central CONUS to start off the \nnew work week. As this trough continues to deepen across the central \nCONUS, a organized cold front from the Southern Plains will push \neast across our CWA Tuesday into Wednesday. Taking a quick look at \nthe parameters, strong deep layer shear will accompany this system. \nFurthermore, 0-3 km bulk shear magnitudes are still pushing into the \n30-50 kt range, though guidance is still showing shear vectors being \nlargely boundary-parallel. Timing in guidance over the past few days \nstill continues to be mostly overnight, which would limit \ninstability. However, there remains some potential for gusty winds \nif there is sufficient instability and storms become oriented \nefficiently with low level shear vectors. Forecast confidence at \nthis point is still low given that this event is still several days \naway. Please continue to monitor this system in the coming days.\n\nWith the upper low cutting off into more of a slow moving bowling \nball type look, clouds may linger behind this system into Wednesday \nand Thursday, but the chance for substantial rain will drop off \nquickly behind the front Wednesday. Temps will see a return to \ncooler mornings and milder days. A few scattered showers will be \npossible across parts of the area by Thursday. Quiet weather \nconditions will prevail across the area heading into Friday. /CR/",
